WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.490
- Hi, I'm Jacqueline Goldsby

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:12.300
I'm chair of the Department
of African-American Studies

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.220
here at Yale and I'm really
grateful and glad to welcome you

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.650
to our department's
colloquium series Endeavors

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.120
and I wanna say a little
bit about this series

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.280
to give you a sense of how it
fits in our department's work

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:32.280
and culture and to bring
you into the conversations

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.253
that we're going to be
starting this semester.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:41.220
Typically, we devote Endeavors
to discuss new scholarship

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:44.690
that's shaping the field of
African-Americans studies.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:48.380
We invite scholars, critics,
artists and activists

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.550
beyond Yale and we also spotlight
the work we're doing here

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.440
on campus to move the conversations

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.970
about the black freedom struggle,

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:00.170
to move the conversations
about all the ways

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:03.240
in which black life effort
has shaped this world.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.590
We bring the newest scholarship
here and we talk about it.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.070
And one of the great things
about this series is that

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:15.680
we focus in typical years
on works in progress.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.070
We wanna see the newest work coming out

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.630
and give it the best possible chance

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.940
in conversation with
our graduate students,

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:24.663
our undergraduate students,

00:01:25.933 --> 00:01:28.290
our colleagues across the
nine different departments

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:32.600
we collaborate with
actively in our PhD program

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.130
and with community members too

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:38.760
who can make the time
to come to our sessions.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.870
We talk about works in progress
so that scholars, activists,

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.520
artists, critics can get feedback

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:49.293
and produce the best work that
they're capable of making.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.820
But this year is unlike any other year

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.493
and you know the conditions we face.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.850
We're facing down a global pandemic

00:02:00.765 --> 00:02:03.100
that's killed over 200,000 Americans

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.763
and over a million
people across the globe.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:10.540
And we know that a disproportionate
number of the US deaths

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:12.440
are occurring in communities of color.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:16.560
We're facing down the
disproportionate killings

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.110
and brutalization and mass
incarcerations of black people

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.453
at the hands of US police
and judicial systems.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:29.470
And as Tuesday night's debate
confirmed absolutely, utterly

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.770
and irrevocably, we're facing down threats

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:38.770
to American democracy that are
stunning but not surprising

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:43.250
given the Republican parties
concerted strategic efforts

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:46.673
to monopolize all branches
and processes of government.

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:50.090
Given these developments,

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:51.810
the African-American Studies faculty

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:56.810
decided to devote Endeavors to
a single theme this semester,

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:00.120
Black Now: Conversations
for the Revolution

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:04.900
and we decided to invite
scholars, activists and artists

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.670
whose work stands up to the challenges

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:11.130
and provides us models for action.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.320
So the questions we're
going to be exploring

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.530
in the sessions we've
planned this semester

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.850
will teach us about these conditions,

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:23.410
teach us about the healthcare
politics of COVID-19,

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:26.340
teach us about the politics
of mass incarceration

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.190
and police brutality and
violence, lethal violence,

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:33.510
teach us about as we will learn today,

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:36.670
voting rights and voting suppression,

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:40.110
but also give us ways to think
about what can we do to act,

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:43.130
what can we do, how can we do it,

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:44.783
what difference can we make?

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.220
So in a minute, I will
introduce my colleague

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:54.620
who will introduce our speakers for today

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:57.510
but I wanna first of all give thanks

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:01.350
and lift up our partnership
with the Center for the Study

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.750
of Race, Indigeneity and
Transnational Migration, RITM,

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:06.710
as it's known here at Yale.

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:09.690
They've been absolutely
wonderful partners with us,

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:14.690
to co-sponsor this series,
helping us stage this event

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.960
for us to convene us together
a community through Zoom.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.290
And I wanna especially
thank the center's director,

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:24.050
Stephen Pitti, who's
a professor of history

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:26.580
and American studies and
ethnicity, race and migration

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:31.000
here at Yale and RITM's
associate director,

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.660
Matthew Tanico, who
worked very, very closely

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:38.270
with our colleagues in
African-American Studies

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:41.320
to organize this event.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:43.220
On the African-American Studies side,

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:45.410
I want to thank Vanessa Epps

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:47.780
who's our newest staff member,

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:50.120
our new senior administrative assistant,

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:54.100
who worked very tirelessly
with Steve Pitti,

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:57.950
with Matt Tanico to
organize the logistics,

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:00.800
thank you so much, Vanessa, for your work.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.110
And I wanna especially thank my colleague,

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:04.913
Professor Crystal Feimster,

00:05:06.317 --> 00:05:09.070
who is one of Yale's most
gifted, brilliant historians

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:13.923
who curated this series
and did so much thoughtful,

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:18.660
she knew who to call, she
knew who to reach out to

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.790
and made those connections happen

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:23.850
and the series that we have going forward

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:26.960
is going to be a brilliant one
and I wanna thank, Crystal,

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:29.280
I just got to give her a shout out here

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.143
for her work, putting this together.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:38.000
My last words here about
our format, we're in Zoom

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.533
so a little Zoom etiquette.

00:05:40.699 --> 00:05:43.980
You might've seen an already
clicked, not hopefully,

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.270
that you're willing to be recorded.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:47.960
We are recording these sessions

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:52.130
because we want to have as
intimate a conversation as we can

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:54.560
in the stage here on campus,

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:56.950
but we're lucky to be
thinking and learning

00:05:56.950 --> 00:05:59.970
with activist, scholars and critics

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:01.730
who are gonna be coming through this term

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:04.180
we want to make these
conversations available

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:05.320
to a broader public

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:11.460
and so we will be posting
them on Yale's YouTube site.

00:06:11.829 --> 00:06:14.730
So we will be recording,

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:19.730
which means please keep your
mics muted, stay on mute

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:23.810
and when it's time for Q and
A, which we want there to be,

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:26.740
we want there to be a robust discussion,

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:29.830
I'll flag now, we're basically
gonna be using the chat

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:32.230
for you to submit your questions

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:36.720
and then we'll present
those to our speaker today

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.390
for deeper conversation.

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:41.600
But a little later on,
Professor Crystal Feimster

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.430
will explain how that function
will work but that's how

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:48.050
we're going to move through
our time together here.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:50.780
So with that, I wanna turn things over

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:52.780
to Professor Crystal Feimster

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:57.500
who will introduce our
guest speaker today.

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:58.333
Thanks.

