that's gonna be on her newest album,

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:09.300
but I'm gonna let her speak
more specifically to that.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:10.610
But I wanna take a moment

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:12.500
before we get into this conversation

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:15.460
to thank the African-American
Studies community

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:18.300
and the RITM community for
everything that they've done

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:19.810
to make this possible.

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:23.980
I wanna thank Emmanuel who
is our tech person today

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:27.010
helping us out but I also
wanna thank everybody

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:28.830
who's agreed to participate,

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:32.070
from my colleagues here
at Yale and beyond.

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:34.980
And I think Jackie pretty
much covered the basis

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:37.260
but I just wanna sort of reaffirm that

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:39.440
and my gratitude to our chair

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:43.370
for also insisting that we
make this happen this semester

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:46.900
because it's important work
and important conversations

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:48.610
that we need to have.

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:52.260
So we are fortunate and really blessed

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:55.930
to be able to our series
with LaTosha Brown

00:11:55.930 --> 00:11:58.673
and Aimee Meredith Cox in conversation.

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:03.650
LaTosha Brown is the co-founder
of Black Voters Matter Fund,

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:05.860
a power building southern based

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:07.980
civic engagement organization

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:09.720
that played an instrumental role

00:12:10.579 --> 00:12:13.280
in the 2017 Alabama US Senate race

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:16.400
and will no doubt play an important

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:19.340
and necessary role in
this coming election.

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:21.270
Ms. Brown is a principal owner

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:23.520
of TrueSpeaks Consulting Inc,

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:27.630
a philanthropy advisory consulting
firm in Atlanta, Georgia.

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:31.700
And for more than 25 years,
she has served as a consultant

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:35.000
and advisor for individual
donors, government,

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:37.440
public foundations, and private donors

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:42.260
who are committed to doing
social justice work so,

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:45.350
but she's also an amazing jazz singer

00:12:46.303 --> 00:12:47.850
and we're gonna get to benefit

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:52.640
from a little bit of her
musical talent I think today.

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:56.570
Aimee Meredith Cox is my
amazing and brilliant colleague

00:12:56.570 --> 00:12:58.980
and friend who's an associate professor

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:00.980
in the Department of
African-American Studies

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:02.500
and Anthropology.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:04.540
Her research and teaching interests

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:08.380
lie at the intersection of
anthropology, black studies

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:11.670
and performance studies
and black feminist theory.

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:15.020
Cox's first monograph,
"Shapeshifters: Black Girls

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:19.080
and the Choreography of
Citizenship," has taught me much

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:22.780
and if you haven't read that
book, you need to read it.

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:24.050
It is a prize winning book,

00:13:24.050 --> 00:13:29.050
she received the 2016
Victor Turner Book Prize

00:13:30.050 --> 00:13:32.750
in Ethnographic Writing
and Honorable Mention

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:37.750
from the 2016 Gloria Anzaldua Book Prize.

