prize and hold on, hold on ♪

00:20:47.660 --> 00:20:50.150
You know, I want to start in those songs,

00:20:50.150 --> 00:20:51.660
just what came up in my spirit

00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:55.590
because I wanted us to get
grounded in the history.

00:20:55.590 --> 00:20:59.070
That we think about, you know
we think about kind of the,

00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:03.400
we have movement as a
cerebral kind of interaction

00:21:03.400 --> 00:21:04.940
that we're thinking about
what were they thinking,

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:06.290
what were their strategies?

00:21:07.179 --> 00:21:08.040
We think about the policy that was shaped,

00:21:08.040 --> 00:21:10.180
we think about the laws
and the Voting Rights Act

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:13.420
that this is the 55th
anniversary of its passage,

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:17.390
but most importantly y'all,
what it was and what grounds me

00:21:17.390 --> 00:21:20.950
at makes me connect it is that
the spirit of the movement

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:23.940
continues to live in me
and I think what it does

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:25.510
is it actually passes down

00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:27.280
through generation and generation.

00:21:27.280 --> 00:21:30.470
I think it transcends time, space,

00:21:30.470 --> 00:21:33.860
that there's something
fundamental that is connected,

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:37.680
I feel connected to, that is
greater than the US government,

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:40.380
that there's something that
is fundamentally greater

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:44.030
than what it seen, those
forces that are beyond

00:21:44.030 --> 00:21:46.710
what I may even think
that I have the strength

00:21:46.710 --> 00:21:47.650
to come up against.

00:21:47.650 --> 00:21:51.770
I believe that my work
is rooted and grounded

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:55.160
as those people before
me, as my ancestors,

00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:57.840
and something that was
greater, that is really driven

00:21:57.840 --> 00:22:01.920
by our love and our power and
our passion around humanity.

00:22:01.920 --> 00:22:04.179
Even when we talk about
the voting rights movement

00:22:04.179 --> 00:22:06.240
and the civil rights movement,

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:08.460
we really think about
that in the context of

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:10.430
what political rights came out of it,

00:22:10.430 --> 00:22:15.430
but fundamentally, people
weren't doing all of that work

00:22:16.380 --> 00:22:17.803
just for the right to vote,

00:22:18.930 --> 00:22:19.763
just to participate in this system,

00:22:19.763 --> 00:22:21.500
they were literally reclaiming

00:22:21.500 --> 00:22:24.000
and literally standing in their agency

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:25.490
around their humanity,

00:22:25.490 --> 00:22:28.470
making sure that their
humanity was recognized

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:30.300
and appreciated and valued.

00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:33.570
And so as I think about the
work of Black Voters Matter

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:36.320
where we're certainly a
power building organization,

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:37.153
it is to.

00:22:41.139 --> 00:22:44.480
What we want to do is literally
be able to remind people

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:46.210
that their power doesn't necessarily come

00:22:46.210 --> 00:22:48.850
from the US government
or any nation state,

00:22:48.850 --> 00:22:51.410
that fundamentally in the
very creation of who they are

00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:56.410
in their spirit, they in fact have value

00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:01.290
and that they in fact have agency

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:04.370
and that any decision
that is made about them

00:23:04.370 --> 00:23:06.110
and their families and the community,

00:23:06.110 --> 00:23:08.350
they have the power to shape that.

00:23:08.350 --> 00:23:12.920
And man, when it does not exist
or when that's threatened,

00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:15.460
they have the right to demand that change.

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:20.460
And so you know my work,
I see our work doing now

00:23:21.040 --> 00:23:26.040
in Black Voters Matter, I so
apologize you all, we are,

00:23:27.320 --> 00:23:28.550
can you hear me?

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:31.300
- Yes, we can hear you, okay, okay.

00:23:31.300 --> 00:23:33.010
- So we are on the bus.

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:35.090
I thoroughly apologize.

00:23:35.090 --> 00:23:38.550
Let me just give some context
for people who are watching.

00:23:38.550 --> 00:23:41.940
We are working and you were
saying that we went to,

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:43.970
in the last 10 days, no,
it was actually half,

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:45.810
that was five days, right?

00:23:45.810 --> 00:23:46.720
It was five days.

00:23:46.720 --> 00:23:48.800
We've had 12 events and we're moving

00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:51.860
and I wanted this discussion

00:23:51.860 --> 00:23:55.030
and being a part of the
discussion is really important.

00:23:55.030 --> 00:23:58.430
As we're doing this discussion,
I want y'all to know

00:23:58.430 --> 00:24:01.020
that I'm also engaged
in the work right now,

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:04.370
that we're actually headed
to, two hours from here,

00:24:04.370 --> 00:24:07.390
we're in North Carolina
and headed to another space

00:24:07.390 --> 00:24:08.540
that we're doing rallies

00:24:08.540 --> 00:24:10.080
and I'll share something about that.

00:24:10.080 --> 00:24:13.230
I am sitting here on the
blackest bus in America

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:17.080
that we go around from
communities to community

00:24:18.100 --> 00:24:19.960
that we spread two things,

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:22.880
that we're committed to spreading
love and building power.

00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:27.260
And so this organization
was founded in 2016

00:24:27.260 --> 00:24:30.250
by Cliff Albright and
I, and what we wanted

00:24:30.250 --> 00:24:33.750
is we wanted to build and
how could we, for years,

00:24:33.750 --> 00:24:35.883
as organizers in the deep South,

00:24:36.841 --> 00:24:38.330
we would sit around and think,

00:24:38.330 --> 00:24:41.600
how can we really be able
to support movement building

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:43.290
and institutional building?

00:24:43.290 --> 00:24:46.530
And so we created this
organization in 2016.

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:47.453
We had the audacity to believe

00:24:47.453 --> 00:24:50.420
that we would come up with
some money from somewhere

00:24:50.420 --> 00:24:51.650
and sometimes you have to move

00:24:51.650 --> 00:24:53.910
because we have been able to be successful

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:56.503
and been able to raise money for a fund.

00:24:56.503 --> 00:24:58.330
We created the Black
Voters Matter Fund first

00:24:58.330 --> 00:25:00.450
and Black Voters Matter
Capacity Building Institute

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:03.090
which is a (c)(3) for the explicit purpose

00:25:03.090 --> 00:25:05.980
of the way that our
theory of change is that

00:25:05.980 --> 00:25:07.530
the way that you build power

00:25:07.530 --> 00:25:09.850
is you build power from the ground up,

00:25:09.850 --> 00:25:11.700
and that the way that you do that is that

00:25:11.700 --> 00:25:13.390
we're not an empire builder,

00:25:13.390 --> 00:25:18.020
which means how about understanding
of how I think about it

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:20.870
is we're not going around the country

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:24.720
trying to get Black Voters
Matter chapters everywhere,

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:26.380
we don't have no interest in that.

00:25:26.380 --> 00:25:28.590
Our goal is to strengthen

00:25:28.590 --> 00:25:31.560
and build the capacity of
existing infrastructure,

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:34.190
that in every community
that there's infrastructure.

00:25:34.190 --> 00:25:36.510
Matter of fact, even if we can go back

00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:38.410
to the enslavement of Africans,

00:25:38.410 --> 00:25:41.290
there was always a system of communicating

00:25:41.290 --> 00:25:44.760
whether the master or the
folks knew, the overseer knew,

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:47.610
there was always a system
of being able to share food,

00:25:47.610 --> 00:25:50.870
to share information, to
even to be able to gather.

00:25:50.870 --> 00:25:52.860
And so there's always an ecosystem,

00:25:52.860 --> 00:25:55.510
there's always a infrastructure
within our communities.

00:25:55.510 --> 00:26:00.470
Our model was how can we be of
service and offering to build

00:26:00.470 --> 00:26:03.490
and support the infrastructure
that's already there

00:26:03.490 --> 00:26:05.940
those organizations,
those groups, those folks

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:08.090
that at the end of the day,

00:26:08.090 --> 00:26:10.070
if something happens in that community,

00:26:10.070 --> 00:26:11.690
they are the first responders.

00:26:11.690 --> 00:26:13.900
That we felt that that's
who needed the support.

00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:16.400
And so we think of ourselves
more like special ops,

00:26:16.400 --> 00:26:17.720
that the work is already going,

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:19.600
the soldiers are already on the ground,

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:22.220
our goal is to come in and show up

00:26:22.220 --> 00:26:25.000
and say what it is that you
need to be able to provide that.

00:26:25.000 --> 00:26:27.827
And so in doing that, there's
a couple of things that we do

00:26:27.827 --> 00:26:29.240
and how we provide that.

00:26:29.240 --> 00:26:31.160
One, we provide resources.

00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:34.170
We provide direct grants to organizations.

00:26:34.170 --> 00:26:39.170
In 2018, we funded over 180
groups almost a million dollars

00:26:40.440 --> 00:26:42.050
that we provided direct resources

00:26:42.050 --> 00:26:44.890
and I wanna talk a
little bit about, Aimee,

00:26:44.890 --> 00:26:47.225
how we did it a little bit different.

00:26:47.225 --> 00:26:49.640
I come out of philanthropy and
I've worked in philanthropy

00:26:49.640 --> 00:26:52.640
for the last 15 years of
my life and Cliff Albright,

00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:54.080
who's also the other co-founder

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:55.950
has worked in philanthropy as well.

00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:57.750
And oftentimes in philanthropy,

00:26:57.750 --> 00:26:59.760
there is a particular kind of way

00:26:59.760 --> 00:27:03.250
that those of us who have
been engaged in philanthropy

00:27:03.250 --> 00:27:06.193
in the social justice world,
it doesn't feel good often.

00:27:07.290 --> 00:27:11.460
In many circumstances you
feel like you gotta swear,

00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:12.670
one, you gotta prove yourself,

00:27:12.670 --> 00:27:13.870
you always got to prove yourself

00:27:13.870 --> 00:27:16.640
that you are worthy to receive resources.

00:27:16.640 --> 00:27:17.587
Two, it often feels like

00:27:17.587 --> 00:27:21.490
you're held at a different
standard, not felt it's true

00:27:21.490 --> 00:27:23.020
that you held at a different standard

00:27:23.020 --> 00:27:24.540
than some of your white counterparts.

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:26.597
And three, if you're not doing the work

00:27:26.597 --> 00:27:29.410
the very way that they
think you should do the work

00:27:29.410 --> 00:27:31.190
because obviously they
know how to movement build

00:27:31.190 --> 00:27:32.640
better than you do, right?

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:36.640
That if you don't do the
work in that context,

00:27:36.640 --> 00:27:38.620
that it's something fundamentally wrong

00:27:38.620 --> 00:27:41.600
and so what winds up happening,
it creates this dance,

00:27:41.600 --> 00:27:44.090
that you have to be
not your authentic self

00:27:44.090 --> 00:27:46.240
or literally talk about
the authentic work.

00:27:46.240 --> 00:27:48.771
You have to create and make yourself,

00:27:48.771 --> 00:27:49.604
you have to contort yourself

00:27:49.604 --> 00:27:52.310
to fit into this kind of criteria

00:27:52.310 --> 00:27:55.090
of which they have set out for it.

00:27:55.090 --> 00:27:57.770
So one of the things that we
did, we wanted to be different

00:27:57.770 --> 00:27:59.700
and so even as we go into communities,

00:27:59.700 --> 00:28:02.940
we do research before we go
into communities, we do advance.

00:28:02.940 --> 00:28:07.940
Before we literally did
anything, Cliff Albright and I

00:28:07.950 --> 00:28:12.440
went around the seven states
in the South and we listen.

00:28:12.440 --> 00:28:15.050
We went from state to state to listen.

00:28:15.050 --> 00:28:19.000
People know what they need,
people know what they want.

00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:21.900
They know power and so fundamentally,

00:28:21.900 --> 00:28:24.332
I think one of the problems is that

00:28:24.332 --> 00:28:25.520
folks are always telling,
particularly black folks,

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:26.353
what to do.

00:28:26.353 --> 00:28:28.250
They wanna tell us what to
do, what we should be doing,

00:28:28.250 --> 00:28:31.330
what we need to think instead
of actually taking the time

00:28:31.330 --> 00:28:35.530
to listen, that there is power
in the listening of people

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:37.640
that can actually shape and tell you

00:28:37.640 --> 00:28:39.040
what it is that they prioritize.

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:42.520
They can also in your hearing,
if you're really listening,

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:45.100
what they're not saying,
you can be able to hear.

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:48.210
And so I'm raising that because

00:28:48.210 --> 00:28:51.050
part of the way that
we've shifted our funding

00:28:51.050 --> 00:28:53.100
and the way that we approached it is that

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:58.608
the groups that we had done work with

00:28:58.608 --> 00:29:00.920
like criteria is one.

00:29:00.920 --> 00:29:03.445
Groups were rooted in community,

00:29:03.445 --> 00:29:05.945
to that community credibility,

00:29:07.673 --> 00:29:09.443
they had community credibility.

00:29:15.590 --> 00:29:19.140
May have been existing
organizations have a 501(c)(3),

00:29:19.140 --> 00:29:20.270
some of 'em were churches,

00:29:20.270 --> 00:29:23.230
some of 'em were
reproductive rights groups,

00:29:23.230 --> 00:29:26.733
some of them were prison reform or,

00:29:27.970 --> 00:29:30.330
some of them were the three ol' sisters

00:29:30.330 --> 00:29:31.880
in the church that just makes sure

00:29:31.880 --> 00:29:34.490
that everything in that
community keeps rolling.

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:37.200
And so fundamentally when we
would identify those groups

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:40.190
and talk to other folks
that actually we were able

00:29:40.190 --> 00:29:41.390
to see those three criteria,

00:29:41.390 --> 00:29:44.780
the first thing we did is we
would give them a mini grant.

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:47.214
No paperwork, no questions.

00:29:47.214 --> 00:29:49.460
It was amazing.

00:29:49.460 --> 00:29:51.407
They would say, "Well, what
did I gotta do for this?"

00:29:51.407 --> 00:29:53.330
And a matter of fact they were suspect,

00:29:53.330 --> 00:29:54.630
like, "Why y'all giving us some money?"

00:29:54.630 --> 00:29:56.750
It was like because we're investing

00:29:56.750 --> 00:29:58.371
in what you have already done.

00:29:58.371 --> 00:29:59.585
It was like, well what
I gotta do for this?

00:29:59.585 --> 00:30:02.240
It was like nothing, that
you've already done the work.

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:06.260
What we wanna do is
give you this investment

00:30:06.260 --> 00:30:08.570
because what we've heard
from your community

00:30:08.570 --> 00:30:10.130
is that you will always serve

00:30:10.130 --> 00:30:12.010
and show up when people need you.

00:30:12.010 --> 00:30:14.440
And so what that did is
it broke the dynamic,

00:30:14.440 --> 00:30:17.840
it shifted the paradigm
of this idea of that

00:30:17.840 --> 00:30:20.540
people have to prove themselves to you.

00:30:20.540 --> 00:30:22.880
And it shifted the paradigm
and broke up this space

00:30:22.880 --> 00:30:25.140
around this grantee-grantor relationship.

00:30:25.140 --> 00:30:27.470
We didn't want a
grantee-grantor relationship

00:30:27.470 --> 00:30:29.330
because there's a power dynamic in that.

00:30:29.330 --> 00:30:32.370
What we wanted is we wanted
a partnership relationship.

00:30:32.370 --> 00:30:34.490
That to recognize that
we are coming to you

00:30:34.490 --> 00:30:36.560
to be supportive to you,

00:30:36.560 --> 00:30:39.200
that you in fact are leading the work,

00:30:39.200 --> 00:30:41.130
that you are in the
fact of who we all need.

00:30:41.130 --> 00:30:43.870
We are dependent on you in
North Carolina and in Alabama

00:30:43.870 --> 00:30:46.570
and Mississippi and so
part of our approach,

00:30:46.570 --> 00:30:48.470
even just initially coming in

00:30:48.470 --> 00:30:49.820
to be able to provide a mini grant,

00:30:49.820 --> 00:30:51.350
it wasn't so much about the money

00:30:51.350 --> 00:30:54.380
but what the money did is
it changed the power dynamic

00:30:54.380 --> 00:30:56.640
in terms of how people
normally get resources

00:30:56.640 --> 00:30:57.970
and so it opened up the space.

00:30:57.970 --> 00:31:00.800
I remember one of the groups
that we're working with

00:31:02.520 --> 00:31:06.570
in Southwest Georgia, he was
so kinda suspect at first

00:31:06.570 --> 00:31:08.000
he said, "You know what?

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:10.710
I don't want a grant, I
just want a megaphone."

00:31:10.710 --> 00:31:12.270
We're like, "You want what?" (laughs)

00:31:12.270 --> 00:31:14.550
He said, "I want a megaphone."

00:31:14.550 --> 00:31:16.490
So we're like, I mean, okay,

00:31:16.490 --> 00:31:19.590
so we bought a megaphone for him.

00:31:19.590 --> 00:31:23.080
Literally the day he got the megaphone,

00:31:23.080 --> 00:31:26.180
he's a leader in the NAACP
in Southwest Georgia,

00:31:26.180 --> 00:31:28.140
one of the counties in Southwest Georgia,

00:31:28.140 --> 00:31:30.100
the day we got the megaphone,

00:31:30.100 --> 00:31:32.500
he organized some of the
folks that he worked with

00:31:32.500 --> 00:31:34.060
and went and took over the city hall.

00:31:34.060 --> 00:31:35.510
We were like, well, if we had known that,

00:31:35.510 --> 00:31:38.530
we would've bought you
two megaphones, right?

00:31:38.530 --> 00:31:41.280
And so part of the, it wasn't so much as

00:31:41.280 --> 00:31:44.920
and after that time, we have
been supporting his work.

00:31:44.920 --> 00:31:49.920
Money matters but really
intention matters most.

00:31:50.020 --> 00:31:53.610
How you show up in this space for groups,

00:31:53.610 --> 00:31:56.700
are you showing up in a way
that you are affirming them

00:31:56.700 --> 00:31:59.020
or are you showing up in a way that says,

00:31:59.020 --> 00:32:02.000
once again you got to
prove to me black people

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:04.420
that you are worthy,
that you are valuable,

00:32:04.420 --> 00:32:06.350
or do you show up in a way that says

00:32:06.350 --> 00:32:08.885
we see the value in what you're doing.

00:32:08.885 --> 00:32:10.540
What we have found is
that the mini grants,

00:32:10.540 --> 00:32:12.330
the mini grants are just a start,

00:32:12.330 --> 00:32:16.340
what we have found that even
in that conversation quickly,

00:32:16.340 --> 00:32:18.743
the conversation is never around money,

00:32:18.743 --> 00:32:20.260
that most of the groups
that we're working with,

00:32:20.260 --> 00:32:23.370
it's not about money because
we changed that dynamic

00:32:23.370 --> 00:32:25.470
from the beginning to let
them know, even for us,

00:32:25.470 --> 00:32:26.700
it's not about money,

00:32:26.700 --> 00:32:29.270
even though that's one of
the things that we offer,

00:32:29.270 --> 00:32:31.700
we wanted to change the paradigm

00:32:31.700 --> 00:32:35.081
in which there's this
monetary exchange and say, no,

00:32:35.081 --> 00:32:36.540
the money is significant

00:32:36.540 --> 00:32:38.280
to the extent that it
helps you do your work.

00:32:38.280 --> 00:32:40.170
What we wanna hear about is your work

00:32:40.170 --> 00:32:41.790
and we'll figure out what we need to do

00:32:41.790 --> 00:32:43.730
and what kind of support you need

00:32:43.730 --> 00:32:45.610
so that we can continue to do that.

00:32:45.610 --> 00:32:50.020
And so even in our funding
process there is a simple,

00:32:50.020 --> 00:32:53.870
so that we can track and keep records,

00:32:53.870 --> 00:32:55.590
there's a simple process now

00:32:55.590 --> 00:32:58.910
that when people fill
out for our mini grants,

00:32:58.910 --> 00:33:03.130
they can get grants up to
$10,000 do their JLTV work

00:33:03.130 --> 00:33:04.680
or their power building work.

00:33:04.680 --> 00:33:06.400
The work that they have deemed

00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:07.890
that's important around power building.

00:33:07.890 --> 00:33:09.970
What's really interesting around it is,

00:33:09.970 --> 00:33:13.920
and this is hard for people
to really conceive of,

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:15.550
just recently some of our partners

00:33:15.550 --> 00:33:17.700
that we know are doing
really, really good work.

00:33:17.700 --> 00:33:20.100
You know, we had given a grant to them,

00:33:20.100 --> 00:33:24.750
one of them in Alabama in
February and so we called

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:27.170
and we said, "Simba, we haven't
gotten anything from you,

00:33:27.170 --> 00:33:29.074
we see you doing all this work,
how are you paying for it?"

00:33:29.074 --> 00:33:30.610
He said, "I'm paying for
it out of my pocket."

00:33:30.610 --> 00:33:32.100
We're like, "Well why are you doing that?"

00:33:32.100 --> 00:33:34.350
And he said, "Because I didn't
wanna come back to y'all,

00:33:34.350 --> 00:33:36.320
I already got some money from y'all."

00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:39.150
I was like, "That's not who we are."

00:33:39.150 --> 00:33:42.540
I'm raising that because
philanthropy has set up a structure

00:33:42.540 --> 00:33:44.380
and a particular kind of culture

00:33:44.380 --> 00:33:47.930
that people constantly feel
like they are some way indebted

00:33:47.930 --> 00:33:50.700
or some way that they
can't come back and ask.

00:33:50.700 --> 00:33:53.240
The way our process is we told them,

00:33:53.240 --> 00:33:55.772
whenever you need
resources, you come to us.

00:33:55.772 --> 00:33:57.640
If we ain't got it, we just
gone tell you we ain't got it

00:33:57.640 --> 00:34:00.040
but I can guarantee you if
there's a will and a way,

00:34:00.040 --> 00:34:00.980
we're gonna help you to figure out

00:34:00.980 --> 00:34:04.680
how we can get that money
even we fundraise it together.

00:34:04.680 --> 00:34:06.440
So even part of our process

00:34:06.440 --> 00:34:10.280
is to really be able to
destroy all of those mechanisms

00:34:10.280 --> 00:34:15.280
that have helped us to feel
marginalized and undervalued

00:34:15.930 --> 00:34:18.760
and so that people have
a, it's so interesting,

00:34:18.760 --> 00:34:20.610
that is like the toughest part

00:34:20.610 --> 00:34:22.810
to really be able to convince folks

00:34:22.810 --> 00:34:26.860
that literally, it's
an open rolling cycle.

00:34:26.860 --> 00:34:30.050
Tell us when you need what you need

00:34:30.050 --> 00:34:33.090
and it is amazing the level of trust,

00:34:33.090 --> 00:34:37.250
the level of expertise, the
way that communities, you know,

00:34:37.250 --> 00:34:39.410
that's why this whole
fallacy that we're hearing

00:34:39.410 --> 00:34:42.820
around people get money, they're abusive.

00:34:42.820 --> 00:34:46.530
We have put so much, we've
wrapped up so much a frame,

00:34:49.290 --> 00:34:53.480
this capitalist frame that centered money,

00:34:53.480 --> 00:34:56.060
that makes people respond to
it in a certain kind of way.

00:34:56.060 --> 00:35:00.810
If you take that out, if
you take the value out

00:35:00.810 --> 00:35:03.790
and literally focus it
on the value on the work

00:35:03.790 --> 00:35:06.900
and the routine of the work,

00:35:06.900 --> 00:35:09.483
it changes the dynamics all together.

00:35:11.440 --> 00:35:14.940
We have had a excellent
experience with our partners

00:35:14.940 --> 00:35:16.900
and we move millions of dollars, right?

00:35:16.900 --> 00:35:18.950
We trust them, they trust us.

00:35:18.950 --> 00:35:22.440
We even know that oftentimes
the money that we give,

00:35:22.440 --> 00:35:24.520
part of the time, I'll give one example

00:35:25.600 --> 00:35:28.100
for people to understand
that it's not about money.

00:35:28.100 --> 00:35:29.510
A good example I wanna share

00:35:29.510 --> 00:35:33.380
is some work that we did in Tennessee.

00:35:33.380 --> 00:35:35.900
In Tennessee there was a
community oversight board.

00:35:35.900 --> 00:35:37.410
There was a group in the community

00:35:37.410 --> 00:35:42.410
that after several murders
of black men and black women

00:35:43.470 --> 00:35:45.010
in that community, they
wanted to come together

00:35:45.010 --> 00:35:47.590
and put a community
oversight board together.

00:35:47.590 --> 00:35:49.013
Now what was interesting is that

00:35:49.013 --> 00:35:52.900
FOP put millions of dollars,
the Fraternal Order of Police

00:35:52.900 --> 00:35:55.660
put millions of, the police
union, millions of dollars

00:35:55.660 --> 00:35:58.450
against this campaign because of course

00:35:58.450 --> 00:36:01.600
they didn't want this oversight board

00:36:01.600 --> 00:36:04.140
'cause in many ways we
know that law enforcement,

00:36:04.140 --> 00:36:05.580
there are many in law enforcement

00:36:05.580 --> 00:36:06.860
that don't want any accountability,

00:36:06.860 --> 00:36:09.430
in particular accountability
to the black community.

00:36:09.430 --> 00:36:12.130
And so when we got involved,
we were just started

00:36:12.130 --> 00:36:15.010
and we got involved and we
were their largest funder

00:36:15.010 --> 00:36:18.430
and I think that we gave them a total of

00:36:18.430 --> 00:36:20.775
somewhere under 30,000,

00:36:20.775 --> 00:36:22.060
I can't remember exactly
what the amount is

00:36:22.060 --> 00:36:23.850
but it was under 30,000.

00:36:23.850 --> 00:36:25.947
They were up against millions of dollars

00:36:25.947 --> 00:36:30.450
and the most powerful union
in lobbying in Tennessee.

00:36:30.450 --> 00:36:32.060
Guess who won?

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:36.330
They won because all they
needed it's not money,

00:36:36.330 --> 00:36:37.620
we think it's oftentimes

00:36:37.620 --> 00:36:39.800
that it is the money
that change the politics,

00:36:39.800 --> 00:36:41.480
no, it's the political wheel.

00:36:41.480 --> 00:36:44.330
If you can get the resources
to people who are organized,

00:36:45.180 --> 00:36:46.870
they are organizations that are doing work

00:36:46.870 --> 00:36:48.690
that you can't even pay
them for their work.

00:36:48.690 --> 00:36:50.630
That fundamentally what they are offering,

00:36:50.630 --> 00:36:53.053
it doesn't even have a tag to it, right?

00:36:54.311 --> 00:36:55.144
There are people that are giving,

00:36:55.144 --> 00:36:58.030
when people are giving out of
their spirit and their passion

00:36:58.030 --> 00:37:01.800
and their belief system, that
is what is transformative.

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:05.120
Money is oftentimes limited
because it's transactional.

00:37:05.120 --> 00:37:07.830
And so while we recognize
that money is needed

00:37:07.830 --> 00:37:10.770
as a tool to help build
the infrastructure,

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:13.720
we also recognize that
it is not the driver,

00:37:13.720 --> 00:37:16.400
it is those things that
create transformation

00:37:17.300 --> 00:37:18.410
that are the driver.

00:37:18.410 --> 00:37:20.290
So Aimee, I know that
was a long way around

00:37:20.290 --> 00:37:22.344
not answering your question.
- No,

00:37:22.344 --> 00:37:24.160
I intentionally opened it that way

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:26.547
because I knew through that,
you would take us there.

00:37:26.547 --> 00:37:28.150
I knew that would just allow you

00:37:28.150 --> 00:37:30.250
to take us all the way there.

00:37:30.250 --> 00:37:32.240
So many lessons, I mean,
that was a whole sermon

00:37:32.240 --> 00:37:33.300
but so many lessons.

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:35.160
Building on what's already there.

00:37:35.160 --> 00:37:37.140
The idea that you have to come into,

00:37:37.140 --> 00:37:39.590
particularly a black
community and change it,

00:37:39.590 --> 00:37:42.560
but we know this, we know
this from our history.

00:37:42.560 --> 00:37:45.550
Life is already there, the
transformation is already there

00:37:45.550 --> 00:37:47.050
so building on that foundation

00:37:47.050 --> 00:37:48.420
and it's not about the money, right?

00:37:48.420 --> 00:37:51.580
The foundation or thereness
that allows for change

00:37:51.580 --> 00:37:54.490
doesn't need money necessarily

00:37:54.490 --> 00:37:57.940
and so to think differently
about how we access resources.

00:37:57.940 --> 00:38:01.900
Another thing I wanna highlight
is how you have traveled,

00:38:01.900 --> 00:38:03.310
now, you're traveling around the country,

00:38:03.310 --> 00:38:05.610
we know this 'cause you're
on this bus with this, sorry,

00:38:05.610 --> 00:38:08.520
janky Wi-Fi, but also you have traveled

00:38:08.520 --> 00:38:10.250
for many different institutions.

00:38:10.250 --> 00:38:11.930
You have traveled through philanthropy,

00:38:11.930 --> 00:38:15.840
which I myself think of one
of the widest most white

00:38:15.840 --> 00:38:18.890
and conservative and
capitalistic structures

00:38:18.890 --> 00:38:22.810
and you have found a way
to renegotiate the terms

00:38:22.810 --> 00:38:25.100
of what philanthropy can
do in our communities.

00:38:25.100 --> 00:38:27.920
So when we think about the
501(c)(2) in our neighborhood

00:38:27.920 --> 00:38:28.850
that's not doing anything,

00:38:28.850 --> 00:38:30.560
everybody knows it's not doing anything,

00:38:30.560 --> 00:38:32.050
but keeps getting grant money

00:38:32.050 --> 00:38:34.060
and you come into that
same community and say, no,

00:38:34.060 --> 00:38:36.260
it's really that
grandmother down the street

00:38:37.117 --> 00:38:38.000
who's taking care of all the children,

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:41.220
who's providing food, that's
where the money needs to go

00:38:41.220 --> 00:38:44.750
and rethinking how we think
about resources in our community

00:38:44.750 --> 00:38:47.950
and what looks like in our communities.

00:38:47.950 --> 00:38:50.540
One thing that I want you to
expand on and move us into

00:38:50.540 --> 00:38:53.170
thinking about the work
specifically right now

00:38:53.170 --> 00:38:57.580
in this moment around black
voters matter is listening.

00:38:57.580 --> 00:39:00.040
And I just have to tell
you as an anthropologist,

00:39:00.040 --> 00:39:03.740
as a black feminist anthropologist,
how jealous I am of you

00:39:03.740 --> 00:39:05.570
on this black bus right now.

00:39:05.570 --> 00:39:07.530
I'm tempted to leave my class--

00:39:07.530 --> 00:39:08.597
- A black bus in America, by the way.

00:39:08.597 --> 00:39:11.210
- And you know, because my whole reason

00:39:11.210 --> 00:39:13.180
for coming into the
discipline of anthropology

00:39:13.180 --> 00:39:16.270
speaking about, you know,
if we're talking about

00:39:16.270 --> 00:39:19.870
colonial projects, you know,
these white institutions,

00:39:19.870 --> 00:39:22.280
you couldn't get much deeper than that

00:39:22.280 --> 00:39:25.140
than anthropology but for me
coming through anthropology

00:39:25.140 --> 00:39:28.000
was about a black feminist
model of being in community

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:29.420
and the way that you talk about.

00:39:29.420 --> 00:39:30.870
You don't just come into a community,

00:39:30.870 --> 00:39:32.550
you research that community,

00:39:32.550 --> 00:39:35.150
you embed yourself in that
community and you listen.

00:39:36.060 --> 00:39:39.430
And so I'm wondering if
you could talk to us about,

00:39:39.430 --> 00:39:44.430
from your listening because we
hear a lot on "CNN", "MSNBC",

00:39:44.530 --> 00:39:47.700
reading blog posts the way
people talk about voting

00:39:47.700 --> 00:39:50.040
in the black community
or black communities,

00:39:50.040 --> 00:39:51.500
the way that black folks are

00:39:51.500 --> 00:39:53.400
or are not thinking about voting,

00:39:53.400 --> 00:39:58.400
the way that Trump has created
the voting electoral process,

00:39:58.980 --> 00:40:02.330
completely de-legitimized
the electoral process

00:40:02.330 --> 00:40:05.450
and how maybe people
are responding to that.

00:40:05.450 --> 00:40:08.330
Our choice or the undecided
vote in the black community

00:40:08.330 --> 00:40:11.040
not being about a vote for Trump or Biden

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:12.750
but whether we vote or not.

00:40:12.750 --> 00:40:15.937
Like these are all sort of
the narrative around voting

00:40:15.937 --> 00:40:17.090
and black communities

00:40:17.090 --> 00:40:20.370
and you are traveling across
the country talking to folks.

00:40:20.370 --> 00:40:23.032
And I'm wondering if you
could give us a sense of

00:40:23.032 --> 00:40:24.840
how the people you are on the ground with,

00:40:24.840 --> 00:40:27.520
you're literally on the ground
with right now in this bus

00:40:27.520 --> 00:40:28.890
traveling through the country,

00:40:28.890 --> 00:40:31.240
how they are imagining their vote,

00:40:31.240 --> 00:40:33.710
how they're thinking
about their relationship

00:40:33.710 --> 00:40:35.780
to the democratic process.

00:40:35.780 --> 00:40:37.360
The kinds of conversations you're having

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:40.490
and what we can learn that
we're perhaps not learning

00:40:41.440 --> 00:40:44.430
when we're watching the news
or through mainstream media.

00:40:44.430 --> 00:40:46.450
Like, what is the real word on the street,

00:40:46.450 --> 00:40:47.760
what are people feeling

00:40:47.760 --> 00:40:49.780
and talking about and struggling with?

00:40:49.780 --> 00:40:52.510
- In essence, what is real in the field?

00:40:52.510 --> 00:40:57.510
And so what I would like to
do, I wanna show a quick clip,

00:40:58.030 --> 00:41:01.640
Crystal, if you can show,
I'm gonna show a quick clip

00:41:01.640 --> 00:41:03.473
and then I'll come back
to answer the question.

00:41:08.964 --> 00:41:12.239
(upbeat music)

00:41:12.239 --> 00:41:13.160
- We got power.

00:41:13.160 --> 00:41:17.993
We're about to get ready and
launch our We Got Power tour.

00:41:20.260 --> 00:41:23.380
- Cliff and I are going on
the blackest bus in America,

00:41:23.380 --> 00:41:24.980
we're hitting the streets again.

00:41:27.390 --> 00:41:29.400
- [Cliff] We're gonna be going
through at least 12 states,

00:41:29.400 --> 00:41:31.593
maybe more, I'm just really excited.

00:41:32.440 --> 00:41:35.907
- Now it's a little bit different
time with this COVID-19,

00:41:35.907 --> 00:41:38.542
we've gotta wear a mask, we've
got to be socially distant

00:41:38.542 --> 00:41:40.073
but we are very committed

00:41:40.073 --> 00:41:42.867
that we've got to get on
the streets and inspire

00:41:42.867 --> 00:41:45.867
and encourage our people in a
way that are socially distant.

00:41:47.520 --> 00:41:50.050
- Ready to hit the road,
ready to see our folks,

00:41:50.050 --> 00:41:52.933
ready to be socially
distant, ready to mask up.

00:41:54.530 --> 00:41:56.120
- [LaTosha] On our way to Pennsylvania,

00:41:56.120 --> 00:41:57.830
we'll be there for two days

00:41:57.830 --> 00:41:59.980
and then we're headed
to Ohio to Cleveland.

00:42:01.460 --> 00:42:03.490
- We're just gonna be just
spreading a lot of love

00:42:03.490 --> 00:42:04.890
and building a lot of power.

00:42:06.524 --> 00:42:08.870
- The very last day,
we're gonna be out here

00:42:08.870 --> 00:42:12.070
on the ground in these streets
because our people need us.

00:42:12.070 --> 00:42:15.610
Can't stop, won't stop.

00:42:15.610 --> 00:42:16.660
- Register to vote.

00:42:18.018 --> 00:42:20.300
You can even request your
online vote by mail ballot

00:42:20.300 --> 00:42:21.980
by clicking the link

00:42:21.980 --> 00:42:24.720
or by scanning our QR
code with your camera.

00:42:24.720 --> 00:42:27.587
Vote early, vote today
'cause we got power.

00:42:41.734 --> 00:42:43.970
- You know the quarantine
in addition to that,

00:42:43.970 --> 00:42:45.870
having the one bus, we actually have two

00:42:45.870 --> 00:42:49.050
and they have some babies
so we have 20 mini buses,

00:42:49.050 --> 00:42:52.330
baby buses that are in 11
states that we're going in

00:42:52.330 --> 00:42:54.685
and I wanna talk about that too

00:42:54.685 --> 00:42:56.090
because I mean it was just a bus, right?

00:42:56.090 --> 00:42:58.810
But it's not the bus that makes the magic,

00:42:58.810 --> 00:43:00.240
it's actually the message.

00:43:00.240 --> 00:43:01.810
And I don't know if you
all were able to see,

00:43:01.810 --> 00:43:04.500
I wanna kind of share one of the incidents

00:43:04.500 --> 00:43:06.950
that happened early on when
we started on every bus,

00:43:06.950 --> 00:43:10.560
every tour, this was our third tour.

00:43:10.560 --> 00:43:13.840
Every tour that we've done
on the bus, you will always,

00:43:13.840 --> 00:43:16.189
you will never see a Black Voters Matter

00:43:16.189 --> 00:43:17.650
and it doesn't have love and power

00:43:17.650 --> 00:43:22.040
and that's because our work
is rooted in those two values,

00:43:22.040 --> 00:43:24.080
love and power, everything that we do

00:43:24.080 --> 00:43:25.330
is in between those two.

00:43:25.330 --> 00:43:27.880
And so when we're talking about the work

00:43:27.880 --> 00:43:29.630
of what we're hearing on the ground,

00:43:29.630 --> 00:43:33.810
we approach the work from a
perspective and a place of

00:43:33.810 --> 00:43:37.230
that everybody has power,
we're just there remind them.

00:43:37.230 --> 00:43:38.320
Not to give them power

00:43:38.320 --> 00:43:40.320
but to remind them that they have power

00:43:40.320 --> 00:43:42.660
and to be able to situate
that and pull it out

00:43:42.660 --> 00:43:45.030
and that everybody is deserving of love

00:43:45.030 --> 00:43:48.060
and sometimes we have to
be reminded of that as well

00:43:48.060 --> 00:43:50.010
and to be reminded that we are loved.

00:43:50.010 --> 00:43:54.690
And so in this process I
think it's really important

00:43:54.690 --> 00:43:57.150
that when we've been talking
to people going on the ground,

00:43:57.150 --> 00:44:01.700
you know, as I said earlier,
I do want people to recognize

00:44:01.700 --> 00:44:06.700
the most powerful tool as an
organizer is listening someone.

00:44:09.507 --> 00:44:10.470
And I didn't say to listen,

00:44:10.470 --> 00:44:15.040
I intentionally said listen
someone and what I mean by that

00:44:15.040 --> 00:44:18.630
it is oftentimes when I
think about, you know,

00:44:18.630 --> 00:44:19.970
think about how many times,

00:44:19.970 --> 00:44:22.190
everybody always got some
advice for black folks.

00:44:22.190 --> 00:44:24.620
What we need to do, what
we need to be thinking.

00:44:24.620 --> 00:44:27.740
I mean, everybody got some
advice about what we doing wrong,

00:44:27.740 --> 00:44:29.830
what we need to be doing, X, Y, Z

00:44:29.830 --> 00:44:32.580
when the truth of the matter,
we have extraordinarily well.

00:44:32.580 --> 00:44:36.253
The fact that right now that
even when I talk about voting,

00:44:37.390 --> 00:44:41.630
just take this, that right
now black people vote

00:44:41.630 --> 00:44:43.610
on par to our white counterparts.

00:44:43.610 --> 00:44:44.940
In addition to that,

00:44:44.940 --> 00:44:47.984
black women actually have
the highest voting turnout

00:44:47.984 --> 00:44:51.200
than any constituency
group in this country.

00:44:51.200 --> 00:44:52.820
The significance of that is

00:44:52.820 --> 00:44:56.010
and like that white voters have received

00:44:56.010 --> 00:44:58.240
the majority of the economic, political

00:44:58.240 --> 00:45:00.900
and social benefits of this system.

00:45:00.900 --> 00:45:04.240
The fact that black folks,
it's only been 50 years,

00:45:04.240 --> 00:45:07.990
when we've seen our vote
be supported and protected

00:45:07.990 --> 00:45:11.460
through the Voting Rights
Act for us to be on par,

00:45:11.460 --> 00:45:13.610
it is actually extraordinary.

00:45:13.610 --> 00:45:15.780
The fact that we have to go through hell

00:45:15.780 --> 00:45:18.883
just to be able to get our
registration, to go to vote.

00:45:19.856 --> 00:45:21.630
I'm saying that because there's
oftentimes this narrative

00:45:21.630 --> 00:45:24.430
as if black folks ain't voting,
yes we are, we're voting

00:45:25.290 --> 00:45:27.760
at the same level as
our white counterparts

00:45:27.760 --> 00:45:30.120
who are reaping the
majority of the benefits.

00:45:30.120 --> 00:45:32.200
So we've got to shift the narrative

00:45:32.200 --> 00:45:34.730
to literally as my
grandmother used to say,

00:45:34.730 --> 00:45:37.520
speak life into people
instead of death in the folks,

00:45:37.520 --> 00:45:39.800
speak life, let's talk about what it is

00:45:39.800 --> 00:45:42.110
not just frame us from a victim

00:45:42.110 --> 00:45:45.680
but frame us from the perspective
of we've got more to do

00:45:46.876 --> 00:45:48.050
and certainly we have to expand

00:45:48.050 --> 00:45:52.110
but also democracy should
not be laid at the feet

00:45:52.110 --> 00:45:53.550
or on the back of black folk.

00:45:53.550 --> 00:45:54.537
When I think about this country

00:45:54.537 --> 00:45:57.237
and I think about the founders
of this country, Aimee,

00:45:58.460 --> 00:46:02.270
the founders of this country
had some brilliant ideas about,

00:46:02.270 --> 00:46:04.400
theoretical, about democracy

00:46:04.400 --> 00:46:06.320
but they certainly were
limited in their vision

00:46:06.320 --> 00:46:09.440
so much so that they
couldn't even see you and I

00:46:09.440 --> 00:46:12.870
or Jacqueline or any
of the others that are,

00:46:12.870 --> 00:46:14.210
many of us on this,

00:46:14.210 --> 00:46:16.180
they didn't even have enough
vision to be able to see

00:46:16.180 --> 00:46:18.410
and recognize our humanity.

00:46:18.410 --> 00:46:20.940
But at the end of the day,
so I consider them, yes,

00:46:20.940 --> 00:46:22.740
they were the founders of this country

00:46:22.740 --> 00:46:25.280
but when I say the founders of democracy,

00:46:25.280 --> 00:46:27.070
I think about those
folks in Selma, Alabama,

00:46:27.070 --> 00:46:28.840
that was on the Edmund Pettus Bridge

00:46:28.840 --> 00:46:30.810
that literally pushed this country

00:46:30.810 --> 00:46:33.000
to be what she needed to be,

00:46:33.000 --> 00:46:35.640
to push this country to
really be able to deal with

00:46:35.640 --> 00:46:37.590
what is the foundation of democracy.

00:46:37.590 --> 00:46:41.210
If the vote is not
afforded to all citizens

00:46:41.210 --> 00:46:44.190
then how can we really call
this truly a democracy?

00:46:44.190 --> 00:46:47.820
And so in that space, I
think we also have to shift

00:46:47.820 --> 00:46:51.083
how we're having this conversation
of what is at stake now,

00:46:52.597 --> 00:46:54.640
we have to shift how we talk about voting

00:46:54.640 --> 00:46:57.570
and we have to shift around
how we engaging around this.

00:46:57.570 --> 00:47:01.490
And so as I talk about
listening, part of the piece is,

00:47:01.490 --> 00:47:03.920
you know I remember a couple of years ago

00:47:03.920 --> 00:47:06.170
when I was first starting
being an organizer,

00:47:07.130 --> 00:47:10.620
I went to a basketball court
and I asked this young man to,

00:47:10.620 --> 00:47:12.520
I was registering people to vote

00:47:12.520 --> 00:47:14.280
and he was very dismissive.

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:16.580
He was like, I'm not
registering with X, Y, Z.

00:47:17.540 --> 00:47:19.524
And I started to try to evangelize him,

00:47:19.524 --> 00:47:21.190
to the tell him how many people had died

00:47:21.190 --> 00:47:24.600
and why he needed to vote and
people have paid the price.

00:47:24.600 --> 00:47:26.660
And I mean, I was just
like passionate with it,

00:47:26.660 --> 00:47:31.657
I was like preaching a sermon
and he said to me calmly,

00:47:34.927 --> 00:47:36.460
"So are you asking me

00:47:37.634 --> 00:47:39.584
that I need to put my faith in a system

00:47:40.780 --> 00:47:43.260
that doesn't even believe in me,

00:47:43.260 --> 00:47:44.960
is that what you're saying to me?"

00:47:46.410 --> 00:47:48.010
What was that gonna say to that?

00:47:48.870 --> 00:47:50.110
I didn't have an ounce at a time.

00:47:50.110 --> 00:47:52.670
It took me about 15 years
to develop my answer.

00:47:52.670 --> 00:47:55.510
I got an answer now, but
it took me about 15 years

00:47:56.892 --> 00:48:00.350
to really sit with that
because truth in what he said.

00:48:00.350 --> 00:48:02.280
And I mean he went on, I mean,
it was a number of things,

00:48:02.280 --> 00:48:03.520
he was a brilliant brother.

00:48:03.520 --> 00:48:06.920
I remember he went on to talk
about they're all folks voting

00:48:06.920 --> 00:48:09.040
and we've elected this and my grandmother

00:48:09.040 --> 00:48:11.010
and them elected these folks
and look at my community,

00:48:11.010 --> 00:48:12.150
look at it right now.

00:48:12.150 --> 00:48:14.690
Like he literally
challenged me on the spot

00:48:14.690 --> 00:48:16.660
and so over a number of years,

00:48:16.660 --> 00:48:19.430
I can still hear that
brother, matter of fact,

00:48:19.430 --> 00:48:24.350
he has been my greatest
teacher around voting

00:48:24.350 --> 00:48:27.440
because it was in my listening to him

00:48:27.440 --> 00:48:30.500
that I had to really deal
and wrestle with the truth

00:48:30.500 --> 00:48:33.710
in what he was saying because
everything he said was true

00:48:34.643 --> 00:48:37.244
and I say that that is
actually in the shaping

00:48:37.244 --> 00:48:38.077
of how I approach the work.

00:48:38.077 --> 00:48:41.240
So when we're going out talking
to folks in the communities

00:48:41.240 --> 00:48:44.850
or on the streets and they
say, "I'm not gonna vote

00:48:44.850 --> 00:48:46.770
'cause I don't believe
that my vote matters

00:48:46.770 --> 00:48:47.923
or my voice matters,

00:48:49.060 --> 00:48:50.620
they gonna do what they wanna do anyway,

00:48:50.620 --> 00:48:53.310
you see how my community operates."

00:48:53.310 --> 00:48:56.070
Instead of me trying to evangelize them

00:48:56.070 --> 00:48:58.730
and to get them to convince
them to believe in a system,

00:48:58.730 --> 00:49:00.970
the first thing that I say is,

00:49:00.970 --> 00:49:04.400
I'm not trying to convince
you to believe in a system,

00:49:04.400 --> 00:49:07.610
I want you to believe
in yourself, number one.

00:49:07.610 --> 00:49:11.120
Number two, I wanna know what
is it that you care about

00:49:11.120 --> 00:49:12.950
'cause I never say that people apathetic,

00:49:12.950 --> 00:49:15.670
I have not had a single conversation

00:49:15.670 --> 00:49:18.180
with any person that didn't
care about something.

00:49:18.180 --> 00:49:20.610
And so instead of the
conversation be around voting,

00:49:20.610 --> 00:49:23.960
the conversation shifts to
what is it that you care about?

00:49:23.960 --> 00:49:26.220
And then when it shifts
to what they care about

00:49:26.220 --> 00:49:29.829
and I listen them, I can understand

00:49:29.829 --> 00:49:30.662
what it is that they wanna see.

00:49:30.662 --> 00:49:32.440
And they usually connect from

00:49:32.440 --> 00:49:34.324
the listening of what they care about

00:49:34.324 --> 00:49:35.784
and I say, "Well, okay, you care about it,

00:49:35.784 --> 00:49:36.730
and you're so passionate about it,

00:49:36.730 --> 00:49:38.680
what is it that you wanna see different?"

00:49:38.680 --> 00:49:40.800
Which then goes into their vision

00:49:40.800 --> 00:49:42.307
and then they'll go into
that vision and say,

00:49:42.307 --> 00:49:47.307
"Well, I wanna see, my cousin got 20 years

00:49:48.320 --> 00:49:52.090
and this other dude cross
town, he only got six months

00:49:52.090 --> 00:49:53.760
and I wanna see something different.

00:49:53.760 --> 00:49:54.780
I wanna see that DA."

00:49:54.780 --> 00:49:55.987
I was like, "Well, what
do you wanna happen?"

00:49:55.987 --> 00:49:58.660
"I want that DA that did
that to him, he got to go"

00:49:58.660 --> 00:50:01.530
Exactly, so if you want
him to go, guess what,

00:50:01.530 --> 00:50:03.200
guess how he gets in power?

00:50:03.200 --> 00:50:04.183
He's elected.

00:50:05.640 --> 00:50:10.030
The point is I make the
connection of voting

00:50:10.030 --> 00:50:13.780
on how it can impact
them getting the decision

00:50:13.780 --> 00:50:16.620
or whatever it is that they
want that they care about.

00:50:16.620 --> 00:50:19.820
Oftentimes we've had voting so far up here

00:50:19.820 --> 00:50:22.910
and part of it is because people really,

00:50:22.910 --> 00:50:24.700
there has been an effort
in this country for us

00:50:24.700 --> 00:50:27.437
not to really lean into
understanding the power of voting.

00:50:27.437 --> 00:50:28.960
And it isn't by accident

00:50:28.960 --> 00:50:31.720
that 45% of the population don't
participate in the process.

00:50:31.720 --> 00:50:35.580
There is an active effort for
people just to be proxies,

00:50:35.580 --> 00:50:36.477
just get somebody to vote for you

00:50:36.477 --> 00:50:37.827
and go about your business.

00:50:39.374 --> 00:50:40.440
We've got to shift the narrative

00:50:40.440 --> 00:50:42.520
from voting being about participation

00:50:42.520 --> 00:50:44.430
to voting being about power.

00:50:44.430 --> 00:50:47.320
And so even in that conversation
what I share with them is

00:50:47.320 --> 00:50:50.130
I will say to them, "I
actually agree with you.

00:50:50.130 --> 00:50:51.700
When you say that you frustrated

00:50:51.700 --> 00:50:53.540
and people been in office
ain't done anything,

00:50:53.540 --> 00:50:55.470
you absolutely right, I'm with you.

00:50:55.470 --> 00:50:58.390
I believe it, I felt it, I
feel the same frustration."

00:50:58.390 --> 00:51:01.970
And so what we have to do is
really be able to show folks

00:51:01.970 --> 00:51:05.320
have a honest conversation
about the limitations of voting

00:51:05.320 --> 00:51:07.360
but also to be able to get them to see

00:51:07.360 --> 00:51:09.630
what power leverage it does have.

00:51:09.630 --> 00:51:12.080
And so if we're bringing
it from a perspective

00:51:12.080 --> 00:51:14.600
of being honest, saying,
yes, you're absolutely right.

00:51:14.600 --> 00:51:17.520
Voting is not the panacea,
I'll be the first one to say,

00:51:17.520 --> 00:51:20.640
voting ain't the panacea,
it ain't the end all be all,

00:51:20.640 --> 00:51:23.415
it's not gonna solve
a lot of our problems.

00:51:23.415 --> 00:51:24.980
It was frustrating to me
when I would hear people say

00:51:24.980 --> 00:51:26.660
someone had gotten murdered,
it was like, well, just vote.

00:51:26.660 --> 00:51:30.180
No, we ain't gotta just vote
but vote is one of the pieces,

00:51:30.180 --> 00:51:32.490
is one vehicle and one leverage to power.

00:51:32.490 --> 00:51:34.960
And so fundamentally we
shift the conversation

00:51:34.960 --> 00:51:37.140
that the conversation isn't
just about participation,

00:51:37.140 --> 00:51:40.120
it's about power and then
not go into this conversation

00:51:40.120 --> 00:51:44.560
to say, I don't know about
you, but LaTosha Brown,

00:51:44.560 --> 00:51:48.160
every decision that is
made about me and my life,

00:51:48.160 --> 00:51:50.303
I need to be a part of that decision.

00:51:51.192 --> 00:51:53.275
How do you feel about it?

00:51:55.501 --> 00:51:56.450
Make a decision about (indistinct),

00:51:56.450 --> 00:51:58.240
well, somebody's gonna be.

00:51:58.240 --> 00:52:00.770
It's just like a basketball
game which is all the time,

00:52:00.770 --> 00:52:03.520
a team is gonna win, the question is

00:52:03.520 --> 00:52:06.970
which team is going to
really reduce the harm

00:52:06.970 --> 00:52:08.130
happening to your community?

00:52:08.130 --> 00:52:10.160
Yes, it might not be
the perfect candidate,

00:52:10.160 --> 00:52:13.130
it might not be the
perfect political party

00:52:13.130 --> 00:52:15.140
but fundamentally and quite quickly,

00:52:15.140 --> 00:52:19.100
it's been very, very rare
that I've had the opportunity

00:52:19.100 --> 00:52:22.580
to vote for the ideal candidate.

00:52:22.580 --> 00:52:26.000
I see voting a tactical strategy

00:52:26.000 --> 00:52:31.000
for a larger goal of what I
seek which is to reduce the harm

00:52:31.330 --> 00:52:34.020
in my community that's
happening right now,

00:52:34.020 --> 00:52:38.390
to hold people accountable,
who are harming my community

00:52:38.390 --> 00:52:41.240
and to also put people
in place and position

00:52:41.240 --> 00:52:42.870
that actually have some alignment

00:52:42.870 --> 00:52:44.820
with the agenda that I
have with my community,

00:52:44.820 --> 00:52:46.300
but I don't stop there.

00:52:46.300 --> 00:52:49.360
While I'm doing that, I am also building

00:52:49.360 --> 00:52:52.820
and building a new framework
for us to collectively vision.

00:52:52.820 --> 00:52:56.300
I am building new networks

00:52:56.300 --> 00:52:59.200
for us to really be able
to amass the kind of power

00:52:59.200 --> 00:53:02.570
that we can actually talk about
the replacement of systems,

00:53:02.570 --> 00:53:05.450
that we can start envisioning
what is the next thing.

00:53:05.450 --> 00:53:07.610
And so my work, when I'm on the streets

00:53:07.610 --> 00:53:09.050
and when we're talking to folks,

00:53:09.050 --> 00:53:10.620
I think there's three points takeaways.

00:53:10.620 --> 00:53:13.060
One, there's an authentic
conversation with folks

00:53:13.060 --> 00:53:15.650
around the limitation but also the power,

00:53:15.650 --> 00:53:16.650
the leverage of voting.

00:53:16.650 --> 00:53:18.850
Two, that we make a specific connection

00:53:18.850 --> 00:53:20.970
around what it is that they care about,

00:53:20.970 --> 00:53:23.150
that how voting can impact that.

00:53:23.150 --> 00:53:24.730
It's hard for you to
get people to care about

00:53:24.730 --> 00:53:25.870
the presidential election

00:53:25.870 --> 00:53:27.650
when they've lost control
of their school board.

00:53:27.650 --> 00:53:29.640
So you start from where people are.

00:53:29.640 --> 00:53:30.750
And then the third thing is

00:53:30.750 --> 00:53:32.520
I think really being able to get people

00:53:32.520 --> 00:53:33.770
to shift the focal point

00:53:34.702 --> 00:53:37.070
so that the focal point
isn't about the candidates

00:53:37.070 --> 00:53:38.890
and it's not about the political parties

00:53:38.890 --> 00:53:40.100
but it's about the people.

00:53:40.100 --> 00:53:42.080
That fundamentally what
the constitution says,

00:53:42.080 --> 00:53:44.080
it doesn't say we the political parties,

00:53:45.022 --> 00:53:46.940
it doesn't say we the
candidates, it say we the people.

00:53:46.940 --> 00:53:50.230
And so over time, I think
we put so much focus

00:53:50.230 --> 00:53:53.160
on how do we amass political parties,

00:53:53.160 --> 00:53:54.590
the power of political parties?

00:53:54.590 --> 00:53:57.050
How do we find the
candidate that's gonna come

00:53:57.050 --> 00:53:59.950
and save us all that we've gotten away

00:53:59.950 --> 00:54:02.780
from the basic foundation of democracy

00:54:02.780 --> 00:54:04.870
that the power always should rest

00:54:04.870 --> 00:54:06.680
and reside with the people.

00:54:06.680 --> 00:54:09.580
- Yes, and I want, you know,
so I'm looking at the chat

00:54:09.580 --> 00:54:10.950
and the chat is mirroring exactly

00:54:10.950 --> 00:54:12.680
what I'm thinking in this moment.

00:54:12.680 --> 00:54:14.990
You mentioned the
skepticism that many voters

00:54:14.990 --> 00:54:16.360
or potential voters have.

00:54:16.360 --> 00:54:18.850
I also wanna talk about the real fears

00:54:18.850 --> 00:54:22.029
and how we can understand
what the threat is.

00:54:22.029 --> 00:54:24.060
So I'll just in terms
of my own experience,

00:54:24.060 --> 00:54:27.310
my family in Ohio, people
are terrified of voting

00:54:27.310 --> 00:54:29.320
and so when we talk
about voter suppression,

00:54:29.320 --> 00:54:31.810
I know that means something
very different in each state

00:54:31.810 --> 00:54:33.690
and it manifests in different ways.

00:54:33.690 --> 00:54:34.650
There are states and folks

00:54:34.650 --> 00:54:37.264
that are more vulnerable than others

00:54:37.264 --> 00:54:38.097
but I'm wondering if
you could speak to that

00:54:38.097 --> 00:54:41.010
and I'm gonna pull up, as
you're thinking about that,

00:54:41.010 --> 00:54:42.840
there's a couple of
comments that speak to that

00:54:42.840 --> 00:54:43.880
really thinking about,

00:54:43.880 --> 00:54:45.640
well when we talk about voter suppression,

00:54:45.640 --> 00:54:47.160
what does that look like?

00:54:47.160 --> 00:54:50.760
How do we understand the
real threat to bodily harm

00:54:50.760 --> 00:54:53.510
and violence that folks are dealing with

00:54:53.510 --> 00:54:55.410
and considering as part of the reason

00:54:55.410 --> 00:54:58.790
to really wanna protect themselves
and then what can we do?

00:54:58.790 --> 00:55:02.790
But I'm gonna read you
a comment from the chat.

00:55:02.790 --> 00:55:06.120
This is Deborah Wells who
does a lot of voting activism.

00:55:06.120 --> 00:55:08.510
She says, "I'm concerned that
when I go to early voting,

00:55:08.510 --> 00:55:10.690
there are white
supremacists at the library

00:55:10.690 --> 00:55:12.080
and I must record their behavior

00:55:12.080 --> 00:55:14.910
and possibly the armed in response.

00:55:14.910 --> 00:55:18.240
Everyone in Florida is
armed, need it come to that."

00:55:18.240 --> 00:55:20.070
And just in conversation with Deborah,

00:55:20.070 --> 00:55:23.197
we have another comment
that's related to that.

00:55:23.197 --> 00:55:25.100
"There are some social science research

00:55:25.100 --> 00:55:27.920
that shows that police
contact not even arrest,

00:55:27.920 --> 00:55:31.790
just contact is a significant
deterrent to black voting.

00:55:31.790 --> 00:55:34.470
Can you talk about how you
think about arming communities

00:55:34.470 --> 00:55:36.030
to resist state violence

00:55:36.030 --> 00:55:39.600
that is actively trying to
disempower or even kill them?"

00:55:39.600 --> 00:55:40.890
So we're having these questions

00:55:40.890 --> 00:55:42.200
that are really thinking about

00:55:42.200 --> 00:55:44.990
kind of the extent of voter suppression

00:55:44.990 --> 00:55:48.050
and what that means as acts of violence

00:55:48.050 --> 00:55:52.140
and how are black
communities processing this,

00:55:52.140 --> 00:55:53.740
how are they thinking about resistance?

00:55:53.740 --> 00:55:55.610
What are your ideas around it?

00:55:55.610 --> 00:55:57.280
And can you outline for us

00:55:57.280 --> 00:56:00.550
what those threads really
look like and what can we do,

00:56:00.550 --> 00:56:02.580
what can we do and what states,

00:56:02.580 --> 00:56:06.263
where should we be putting
our energy and our attention?

00:56:13.080 --> 00:56:14.530
- I'm back.

00:56:14.530 --> 00:56:17.984
- Okay, alright, LaTosha did you hear--

00:56:17.984 --> 00:56:18.841
- I heard the question.

00:56:18.841 --> 00:56:20.923
- Okay.
I heard the question.

00:56:20.923 --> 00:56:21.756
- [Aimee] Okay, okay.

00:56:21.756 --> 00:56:24.330
- I am on the back
roads of North Carolina,

00:56:24.330 --> 00:56:27.227
welcome to the bus, y'all. (laughs)

00:56:31.186 --> 00:56:33.620
Let me share a personal experience

00:56:33.620 --> 00:56:36.103
that happened two days ago.

00:56:38.351 --> 00:56:42.670
Two days ago ago, I
wanted to show the bus.

00:56:42.670 --> 00:56:47.670
We've got, you know,
it's a moving billboard

00:56:48.760 --> 00:56:51.680
and you may not have been
able to see the details

00:56:51.680 --> 00:56:53.560
but the way that on the bus it has,

00:56:53.560 --> 00:56:56.660
we got power in huge, huge letters.

00:56:56.660 --> 00:57:00.470
It has black fist, love, power,

00:57:00.470 --> 00:57:02.650
black people all around the bus.

00:57:02.650 --> 00:57:04.910
It's about us all of these messages

00:57:04.910 --> 00:57:07.450
that are affirming
message for black people

00:57:08.536 --> 00:57:10.500
and even the we got power in the middle of

00:57:10.500 --> 00:57:12.440
it's a black fist rays.

00:57:12.440 --> 00:57:16.930
And so on the highway, it
gets its own share of love

00:57:16.930 --> 00:57:19.540
and stares and everything in between

00:57:19.540 --> 00:57:24.413
but we rented a, Cliff and
I, because of COVID-19,

00:57:25.420 --> 00:57:27.087
we had decided that we
would stay in an Airbnb.

00:57:27.087 --> 00:57:30.610
And so we went to Cleveland,
we left, we went to Cleveland

00:57:30.610 --> 00:57:33.150
we went to stay in an Airbnb
and normally what will happen

00:57:33.150 --> 00:57:38.150
is there is a, the bus
will go to the hotel

00:57:38.870 --> 00:57:40.880
'cause the buses stays at the hotel

00:57:42.060 --> 00:57:44.930
and then there's our
security team or runner team

00:57:44.930 --> 00:57:47.630
will take us back to the Airbnb.

00:57:47.630 --> 00:57:51.240
Well, on this particular trip
what wound up happening was

00:57:54.600 --> 00:57:56.040
the bus went directly

00:57:56.040 --> 00:57:58.920
because we already had
been driving all day,

00:57:58.920 --> 00:58:02.030
the bus went directly to the house.

00:58:02.030 --> 00:58:04.210
We went to the Airbnb at the house

00:58:04.210 --> 00:58:08.960
and because we were doing a
training for groups in Texas,

00:58:08.960 --> 00:58:11.200
we set out in front of the
house for about 20 minutes,

00:58:11.200 --> 00:58:13.760
did the training went inside.

00:58:13.760 --> 00:58:17.243
We were with our videographer
that was doing some interview.

00:58:18.993 --> 00:58:20.160
So as we went,

00:58:21.583 --> 00:58:26.440
I went upstairs and kept
working on our plan for Detroit,

00:58:26.440 --> 00:58:29.430
we were starting to plan
our trip for Detroit.

00:58:29.430 --> 00:58:34.160
Cliff, my co-partner was
downstairs doing an interview

00:58:34.160 --> 00:58:35.470
and while I was up there on the phone,

00:58:35.470 --> 00:58:36.720
I could smell something burning

00:58:36.720 --> 00:58:40.120
but what I thought was
that there were some wires

00:58:40.120 --> 00:58:43.347
that may have been loose
in the top of that building

00:58:43.347 --> 00:58:45.580
and for whatever reason,

00:58:45.580 --> 00:58:47.790
silly me, I didn't get
up to go check it out.

00:58:47.790 --> 00:58:50.020
And so about five minutes later,

00:58:50.020 --> 00:58:51.850
we hear this bang on the door

00:58:51.850 --> 00:58:55.070
like they're trying to bang
the door down and we're like,

00:58:55.070 --> 00:58:56.560
what is happening, we're starling.

00:58:56.560 --> 00:58:58.770
And so we go to the door,
Cliff go to the door

00:58:58.770 --> 00:59:01.240
'cause I'm upstairs and
this woman is screaming

00:59:01.240 --> 00:59:03.580
and she says, "Get out,
your house is on fire.

00:59:03.580 --> 00:59:04.413
Your house is on fire."

00:59:04.413 --> 00:59:05.940
We're like, "What are you talking about?"

00:59:05.940 --> 00:59:07.930
And sure enough and we could smell it

00:59:07.930 --> 00:59:10.040
and so I run downstairs, Cliff goes

00:59:10.040 --> 00:59:11.640
and we look in the back of the house

00:59:11.640 --> 00:59:13.000
and all we see is fire.

00:59:13.000 --> 00:59:18.000
And so he goes inside,
he gets a bucket of water

00:59:18.650 --> 00:59:20.260
and he goes to try to take it out.

00:59:20.260 --> 00:59:22.854
Now, mind you it's raining outside

00:59:22.854 --> 00:59:23.687
so we're like, well,
how did the fire start

00:59:23.687 --> 00:59:25.010
and it's raining outside.

00:59:25.010 --> 00:59:27.780
And so we call the fire department, 911,

00:59:27.780 --> 00:59:30.850
the fire department comes
and they extinguish the fire

00:59:30.850 --> 00:59:33.414
and then they start asking us questions.

00:59:33.414 --> 00:59:35.970
And what we later found out,

00:59:35.970 --> 00:59:40.000
there's a recyclable
bin that's by the house,

00:59:40.000 --> 00:59:42.760
the garbage can that's
by the individual house

00:59:42.760 --> 00:59:47.760
that was on fire.

00:59:47.790 --> 00:59:49.260
And so we don't know,

00:59:49.260 --> 00:59:51.110
at first the fire marshal was asking us,

00:59:51.110 --> 00:59:52.510
well, was there something in there,

00:59:52.510 --> 00:59:53.343
were you all smoking?

00:59:53.343 --> 00:59:54.630
And we were like, no, we weren't smoking.

00:59:54.630 --> 00:59:58.270
We went in the house,
we didn't do anything.

00:59:58.270 --> 01:00:01.160
And later, and then I
wound up telling him,

01:00:01.160 --> 01:00:03.490
it was interesting two
things that stuck out to me.

01:00:03.490 --> 01:00:05.840
As we were talking I said
and he was like well,

01:00:10.600 --> 01:00:12.330
'cause it was (indistinct) outside,

01:00:12.330 --> 01:00:15.920
so we moved our campaign bus.

01:00:15.920 --> 01:00:18.430
I was like and the bus
has some messages on it

01:00:18.430 --> 01:00:21.250
like the word black that some
people may find offensive.

01:00:21.250 --> 01:00:23.700
And so when I said that to him,

01:00:23.700 --> 01:00:25.200
it was interesting his response

01:00:25.200 --> 01:00:27.710
'cause his response was
almost like, oh, I get it.

01:00:27.710 --> 01:00:30.772
Like oh okay, then that
makes sense. (laughs)

01:00:30.772 --> 01:00:32.380
And I was like, well damn,
we can't even say black, huh?

01:00:32.380 --> 01:00:35.510
But anyway and he said, okay

01:00:35.510 --> 01:00:37.270
and they brought the arson team.

01:00:37.270 --> 01:00:40.140
What was the second thing
that was interesting is,

01:00:40.140 --> 01:00:42.090
across the street, they
went across the street

01:00:42.090 --> 01:00:44.807
'cause they were trying to get footage

01:00:44.807 --> 01:00:46.820
to see who had set the fire
'cause the fence burned,

01:00:46.820 --> 01:00:49.350
it could have been a
really bad circumstance.

01:00:49.350 --> 01:00:51.110
At this point we had
decided we were gonna leave

01:00:51.110 --> 01:00:55.200
and go to a hotel and he
went across the street

01:00:55.200 --> 01:00:59.000
and there happened to be a
black family across the street

01:00:59.000 --> 01:01:00.730
and there was a woman upstairs

01:01:00.730 --> 01:01:02.567
that was yelling out the window saying,

01:01:02.567 --> 01:01:05.660
"I am so tired of this,
I am so tired of this.

01:01:05.660 --> 01:01:08.410
Every time somebody
black rents that house,

01:01:08.410 --> 01:01:09.610
they call the police on 'em.

01:01:09.610 --> 01:01:12.350
Every time they keep
harassing those people."

01:01:12.350 --> 01:01:14.240
And so the man downstairs

01:01:14.240 --> 01:01:16.070
who I don't know what
her relationship with,

01:01:16.070 --> 01:01:18.460
but I could tell they were family,

01:01:18.460 --> 01:01:21.480
he kept telling her, "Go
back in, go back in."

01:01:21.480 --> 01:01:23.600
And so I decided to ask him

01:01:23.600 --> 01:01:26.170
but I could tell that there
was some fear from him

01:01:26.170 --> 01:01:28.570
and I just kinda asked
him quietly, I said,

01:01:30.347 --> 01:01:33.770
"Do you have some rental
problems in this neighborhood?"

01:01:33.770 --> 01:01:36.770
I didn't realize we were
staying in a white neighborhood.

01:01:36.770 --> 01:01:38.990
And he said, "Yes."

01:01:38.990 --> 01:01:40.176
I said, "Do you think

01:01:40.176 --> 01:01:41.920
somebody tried to set the house on fire?"

01:01:41.920 --> 01:01:44.429
He said, "Why else?"

01:01:44.429 --> 01:01:45.620
And I knew he was talking in code to me.

01:01:45.620 --> 01:01:47.170
He said, "Why else?"

01:01:47.170 --> 01:01:50.670
And all of the fire chief
and the arson investigator

01:01:50.670 --> 01:01:55.670
is standing there, "Why else
would a trash can by the house

01:01:56.610 --> 01:01:59.237
catch on fire in the middle of the rain?"

01:01:59.237 --> 01:02:00.873
And he just looked at me.

01:02:02.046 --> 01:02:03.630
I said, "Okay, okay.

01:02:03.630 --> 01:02:06.610
Do you think that we
probably should leave?"

01:02:06.610 --> 01:02:08.650
He said, "Yes."

01:02:08.650 --> 01:02:10.680
I was like, "Okay, got it."

01:02:10.680 --> 01:02:12.410
He said everything I needed to know.

01:02:12.410 --> 01:02:16.210
We knew, you know, it's
interesting because

01:02:17.270 --> 01:02:20.890
I think of myself as
someone who is courageous

01:02:22.520 --> 01:02:24.293
but racism is traumatic.

01:02:25.340 --> 01:02:26.920
This just happened two days ago.

01:02:26.920 --> 01:02:31.600
We've had I think 11 events
since then in the same city

01:02:31.600 --> 01:02:35.800
the next day we had eight,
I'm still shook to be honest.

01:02:35.800 --> 01:02:39.170
I'm sitting here, the idea
that I'm thinking that

01:02:39.170 --> 01:02:40.430
just because we got a bus

01:02:40.430 --> 01:02:42.970
that has black voters matter on it,

01:02:42.970 --> 01:02:47.700
that you would try to
burn us up and kill us.

01:02:47.700 --> 01:02:49.860
Just the reality that it is real,

01:02:49.860 --> 01:02:52.330
and I can go on with other stories.

01:02:52.330 --> 01:02:56.770
Part of, you won't hear a
lot of those stories out

01:02:56.770 --> 01:02:59.330
because I've made kind
of the strategic choice

01:02:59.330 --> 01:03:02.450
that sometimes I think
violence begets violence

01:03:02.450 --> 01:03:05.100
and so a lot of times
when you put it out there,

01:03:05.100 --> 01:03:07.550
it creates all these copycats.

01:03:07.550 --> 01:03:10.120
And so I don't know if that's
the best choice or the,

01:03:10.120 --> 01:03:11.910
I don't know if that's
a good choice or not,

01:03:11.910 --> 01:03:13.690
but we don't publicize it as much.

01:03:13.690 --> 01:03:14.910
I mean, just even a month ago,

01:03:14.910 --> 01:03:16.810
I was in Louisville, Kentucky.

01:03:16.810 --> 01:03:20.020
I went there to stand with the
families of Breonna Taylor.

01:03:20.020 --> 01:03:22.810
We went to a gospel
concert, we did a training

01:03:22.810 --> 01:03:26.750
and we were leaving, a police officer,

01:03:26.750 --> 01:03:30.490
well, I'm not sure if
he was a police officer,

01:03:30.490 --> 01:03:31.470
may have been a state trooper,

01:03:31.470 --> 01:03:35.660
an officer took a picture of the bus,

01:03:35.660 --> 01:03:39.943
posted it in some white
groups on Facebook.

01:03:42.620 --> 01:03:43.870
My brother's mother is white

01:03:43.870 --> 01:03:46.080
and friends with someone in Kentucky,

01:03:46.080 --> 01:03:49.450
literally put that there
were riders and thugs

01:03:49.450 --> 01:03:53.340
that were coming Kentucky
to tack Kentucky down

01:03:53.340 --> 01:03:56.720
and the list went on and
on and on of people saying,

01:03:56.720 --> 01:03:59.610
actively looking for the
bus, how they could hurt us,

01:03:59.610 --> 01:04:02.540
how they need to cut the tires,

01:04:02.540 --> 01:04:07.073
how they needed to do a
road block to really bring,

01:04:10.210 --> 01:04:14.717
after that even a intern just
go through the hate emails

01:04:18.110 --> 01:04:20.272
and the messages that we get.

01:04:20.272 --> 01:04:22.430
Because while we're doing this work

01:04:23.460 --> 01:04:26.360
in the spirit of the power
in love, hate is real.

01:04:26.360 --> 01:04:31.290
We have pooled up into
places down in South Georgia

01:04:31.290 --> 01:04:33.387
where when we pulled up,
just like in the '60s,

01:04:33.387 --> 01:04:36.840
the folks in our face will flip the sign

01:04:36.840 --> 01:04:39.380
that say we're closed to
keep us from getting gas

01:04:39.380 --> 01:04:40.850
or something in a convenience store.

01:04:40.850 --> 01:04:43.133
I'm talking about in 2018, 2020.

01:04:45.597 --> 01:04:48.550
I've got stories but I can
also tell you the story

01:04:48.550 --> 01:04:50.600
about when we went to Randolph County

01:04:51.820 --> 01:04:53.970
and when we're in Randolph County,

01:04:53.970 --> 01:04:56.793
this woman who was a elder,

01:04:57.896 --> 01:04:59.750
they were closing the polling site

01:04:59.750 --> 01:05:02.867
and we had a mass meeting
and as we were walking out,

01:05:03.750 --> 01:05:05.390
we walk out, she walks out with her cane

01:05:05.390 --> 01:05:07.080
and I'm walking kinda assist her

01:05:07.080 --> 01:05:10.110
and she looks up at the
bus, I'm actually moved now,

01:05:10.110 --> 01:05:12.307
she looks up at the bus and she said,

01:05:12.307 --> 01:05:14.830
"Baby does that by say
black voters matter?"

01:05:14.830 --> 01:05:17.590
And I said, "Yes, Ma'am because we do,"

01:05:17.590 --> 01:05:19.250
and she started crying.

01:05:19.250 --> 01:05:23.710
I know why she cried and
I'm saying that because

01:05:24.840 --> 01:05:29.470
we cannot act like this
election is another election.

01:05:29.470 --> 01:05:31.870
Y'all this ain't enough just
about two different candidates,

01:05:31.870 --> 01:05:33.530
we are talking about fascism,

01:05:33.530 --> 01:05:36.840
we are talking about the
president of the United States

01:05:36.840 --> 01:05:38.810
that literally gave license

01:05:38.810 --> 01:05:41.510
to a white nationalist supremacist group

01:05:41.510 --> 01:05:45.920
from the debate stage and we're
operating like it's normal,

01:05:45.920 --> 01:05:49.620
we're operating as like it's acceptable.

01:05:49.620 --> 01:05:51.180
And so I share that

01:05:51.180 --> 01:05:54.060
because when we're talking
about moving around in the bus

01:05:54.060 --> 01:05:56.070
and when we're talking about
fighting for democracy,

01:05:56.070 --> 01:05:59.890
literally we've had to
hire security to go.

01:05:59.890 --> 01:06:02.330
I myself, like I'm not just going around.

01:06:02.330 --> 01:06:05.850
People can see us and we're that's real,

01:06:05.850 --> 01:06:08.230
we're excited and we're doing the work,

01:06:08.230 --> 01:06:11.400
but even for us just to have the audacity

01:06:11.400 --> 01:06:12.860
to say black voters matter,

01:06:12.860 --> 01:06:17.050
there are people that seek
to do us harm, to scare us.

01:06:17.050 --> 01:06:22.050
I personally, even at my own
home two maybe a month ago,

01:06:23.320 --> 01:06:28.320
received a package that was
handwritten to me to my home,

01:06:30.140 --> 01:06:31.640
even though we've gotten my address

01:06:31.640 --> 01:06:33.700
scrubbed from the Internet from Russia

01:06:33.700 --> 01:06:37.913
so much so that the FBI came to my house.

01:06:39.458 --> 01:06:42.320
They got the package, all it
was, what we found out was

01:06:42.320 --> 01:06:44.550
it was a little toy like a squishy toy

01:06:44.550 --> 01:06:47.200
that had one word that
said Borowski on it.

01:06:47.200 --> 01:06:48.590
There wasn't anything dangerous.

01:06:48.590 --> 01:06:50.858
They brought the HAZMAT team out.

01:06:50.858 --> 01:06:51.691
They took it to the crime lab

01:06:51.691 --> 01:06:55.160
but they spent a whole lot of
time and energy and resources

01:06:55.160 --> 01:06:58.680
to send something to me that
does not have any value.

01:06:58.680 --> 01:07:01.047
I'm raising this because
when you are seeing people,

01:07:01.047 --> 01:07:02.960
and I'm not saying this about me,

01:07:02.960 --> 01:07:04.173
I'm saying this that
when you're seeing people

01:07:04.173 --> 01:07:06.610
who are on the front lines every day

01:07:06.610 --> 01:07:08.050
that are literally out there,

01:07:08.050 --> 01:07:11.380
they are literally risking their lives,

01:07:11.380 --> 01:07:13.830
that we are in a particular
kind of environment

01:07:13.830 --> 01:07:15.630
that I don't think we're taking seriously.

01:07:15.630 --> 01:07:17.060
That we're not understanding

01:07:17.060 --> 01:07:19.220
that when Trump sends the dog whistles

01:07:19.220 --> 01:07:21.763
to white supremacists,
you can go to my page,

01:07:22.660 --> 01:07:24.890
we've got screenshots

01:07:24.890 --> 01:07:27.760
of where there are white
supremacist groups that literally,

01:07:27.760 --> 01:07:30.680
a motorcycle group just
the other day, yesterday

01:07:30.680 --> 01:07:32.490
that saw the bus at a stop

01:07:32.490 --> 01:07:34.830
and said, "We pulled off
'cause we got scared."

01:07:34.830 --> 01:07:38.210
I mean just, the bottom line
is there is an environment

01:07:38.210 --> 01:07:40.330
that part of what is voter suppression

01:07:40.330 --> 01:07:41.720
has been an environment of fear.

01:07:41.720 --> 01:07:44.110
And so when we're doing this work,

01:07:44.110 --> 01:07:46.210
we have to recognize what is at stake.

01:07:46.210 --> 01:07:49.150
This isn't just about an
election and about candidates,

01:07:49.150 --> 01:07:50.470
this is literally around

01:07:50.470 --> 01:07:53.350
how are we going to shift
the culture of this country

01:07:53.350 --> 01:07:56.800
that we uproot racism and
structural racism in a way

01:07:56.800 --> 01:08:01.350
that traumatizes, creates
harm and discouragement

01:08:01.350 --> 01:08:03.293
to millions of people in this country.

01:08:04.330 --> 01:08:09.330
- Hmm, okay, I first wanna say
