01:23:24.580 --> 01:23:26.640
that fundamentally in a democracy, people,

01:23:26.640 --> 01:23:30.700
all people I believe
should have the ability

01:23:30.700 --> 01:23:32.440
to be able to make a choice and a decision

01:23:32.440 --> 01:23:33.660
about decisions made about them

01:23:33.660 --> 01:23:35.480
including those that
have been incarcerated.

01:23:35.480 --> 01:23:38.000
And so if you look at the work,
I'm just gonna lead you all

01:23:38.000 --> 01:23:40.710
to the work of Desmond
Meade down in Florida,

01:23:40.710 --> 01:23:42.520
some of the work that
I did over 10 years ago

01:23:42.520 --> 01:23:44.960
was I actually started
the Alabama Coalition

01:23:44.960 --> 01:23:45.793
to restore the vote.

01:23:45.793 --> 01:23:47.910
And so I think there's
a critical component

01:23:47.910 --> 01:23:50.670
around when you look at who's
on the fringes of democracy,

01:23:50.670 --> 01:23:52.620
that's actually what points to you

01:23:52.620 --> 01:23:55.151
what will strengthen democracy.

01:23:55.151 --> 01:23:55.984
And so I think fundamentally,

01:23:55.984 --> 01:23:58.240
please look into the work of
Desmond Meade down in Florida.

01:23:58.240 --> 01:24:03.240
Second thing is when we're
talking about the South,

01:24:03.360 --> 01:24:05.380
so y'all just follow me with this.

01:24:05.380 --> 01:24:07.840
When we think about the
financial center in this country,

01:24:07.840 --> 01:24:09.090
what do you think about?

01:24:09.090 --> 01:24:09.923
Wall Street.

01:24:09.923 --> 01:24:12.622
When you think about the
political policy center

01:24:12.622 --> 01:24:14.380
in this country, what do you
think about? Washington DC.

01:24:14.380 --> 01:24:15.880
When you think about
the entertainment center

01:24:15.880 --> 01:24:17.300
in this country, what do you think about?

01:24:17.300 --> 01:24:19.010
Hollywood. You don't even
have to think twice about it.

01:24:19.010 --> 01:24:21.150
When you think about the
roots of white patriarchy

01:24:21.150 --> 01:24:22.820
and supremacy in this country,
what do you think about?

01:24:22.820 --> 01:24:24.230
The South, that's right, you're right.

01:24:24.230 --> 01:24:28.800
The bottom line is when
we're talking about who is,

01:24:28.800 --> 01:24:32.070
you can be as blue as you
wanna be in California

01:24:32.070 --> 01:24:35.480
and in New York but who is
running the country right now

01:24:35.480 --> 01:24:36.920
and setting policy for the country?

01:24:36.920 --> 01:24:39.870
It's actually southern senators.

01:24:39.870 --> 01:24:41.310
Mitch McConnell is from where?

01:24:41.310 --> 01:24:42.690
Kentucky, right?

01:24:42.690 --> 01:24:44.140
Lindsey Graham is from where?

01:24:44.140 --> 01:24:45.230
South Carolina.

01:24:45.230 --> 01:24:46.880
And so because we've advocated,

01:24:46.880 --> 01:24:48.140
when we're talking about the South,

01:24:48.140 --> 01:24:51.160
welcome to the South y'all, we
all experiencing it right now

01:24:51.160 --> 01:24:52.840
that fundamentally what
we're talking about

01:24:52.840 --> 01:24:56.310
is shifting the national
discourse around race

01:24:56.310 --> 01:24:58.530
and other issues and policy priorities.

01:24:58.530 --> 01:25:00.620
We're allowing southern senators

01:25:01.874 --> 01:25:03.970
to actually take control of that process.

01:25:03.970 --> 01:25:05.180
They are literally running the country.

01:25:05.180 --> 01:25:06.540
It wasn't by accident

01:25:06.540 --> 01:25:08.820
that Trump's very first
rally was in Alabama.

01:25:08.820 --> 01:25:11.580
And so as long as the roots of racism

01:25:11.580 --> 01:25:14.013
allowed to live unchallenged in the South,

01:25:14.013 --> 01:25:17.004
that we will continue to
see what we're seeing.

01:25:17.004 --> 01:25:17.837
So when we're talking about,

01:25:17.837 --> 01:25:19.150
there will be no progressive America

01:25:19.150 --> 01:25:22.500
unless we uproot and strengthen
and work in alignment

01:25:22.500 --> 01:25:24.670
with folks in the South,
that's just the truth.

01:25:24.670 --> 01:25:26.040
We've got the receipts to show that.

01:25:26.040 --> 01:25:28.420
And so fundamentally what I say is

01:25:28.420 --> 01:25:30.210
the South is red until it ain't.

01:25:30.210 --> 01:25:32.720
That fundamentally when
you look at the people,

01:25:32.720 --> 01:25:35.600
the numbers that are in North
Carolina and South Carolina

01:25:35.600 --> 01:25:38.330
and Alabama, the reason
why the voter suppression

01:25:38.330 --> 01:25:40.260
is so great is because

01:25:40.260 --> 01:25:42.490
when you look at who's moving down South

01:25:42.490 --> 01:25:44.090
and you look up at the demographics

01:25:44.090 --> 01:25:47.120
and the demographic shifts,
that that's where our power is.

01:25:47.120 --> 01:25:48.250
So when I'm talking about,

01:25:48.250 --> 01:25:50.777
we're talking about building
a progressive movement

01:25:50.777 --> 01:25:53.585
in this country, that you
uproot the structural racism

01:25:53.585 --> 01:25:56.940
in the South, you challenge
that, if you change the South,

01:25:56.940 --> 01:26:00.130
as goes to South goes
nation point blank period.

01:26:00.130 --> 01:26:02.900
The last and the final point is

01:26:02.900 --> 01:26:06.430
I will say that black women
have always been on the vanguard

01:26:06.430 --> 01:26:07.750
of fighting for democracy.

01:26:07.750 --> 01:26:09.280
We even stood 100 years ago

01:26:09.280 --> 01:26:10.774
in the women's suffrage movement

01:26:10.774 --> 01:26:13.380
with our white counterparts.

01:26:13.380 --> 01:26:14.540
Like let's be honest,

01:26:14.540 --> 01:26:16.930
what most don't most
people don't know is that

01:26:16.930 --> 01:26:18.230
the abolitionists movement

01:26:19.660 --> 01:26:21.760
actually birthed the
women's suffrage movement,

01:26:21.760 --> 01:26:24.414
it grew out of the abolitionists movement.

01:26:24.414 --> 01:26:25.247
And that in that context,

01:26:25.247 --> 01:26:29.440
that while black women were
actually on the vanguard

01:26:29.440 --> 01:26:30.600
of supporting that,

01:26:30.600 --> 01:26:33.180
that literally it was over 50 years later

01:26:33.180 --> 01:26:35.010
before we secured our rights.

01:26:35.010 --> 01:26:38.130
And so just as we stood with
our white women counterparts,

01:26:38.130 --> 01:26:41.660
it's gonna be interesting to
see whether they stand with us,

01:26:41.660 --> 01:26:43.950
and I think that we've got
to put that strategy as well

01:26:43.950 --> 01:26:47.210
that fundamentally what black
women have been able to do,

01:26:47.210 --> 01:26:49.340
that in the midst of, we understand,

01:26:49.340 --> 01:26:53.420
we sit at a particular
intersection of sexism and racism

01:26:53.420 --> 01:26:55.560
and our ability to be
able to navigate those

01:26:55.560 --> 01:26:56.850
through those worlds

01:26:56.850 --> 01:26:59.103
and our ability to tap into our humanity,

01:27:00.110 --> 01:27:02.730
I always say, my grandmother told me that,

01:27:02.730 --> 01:27:05.170
my great-grandmother was a wet nurse,

01:27:05.170 --> 01:27:08.360
like who can have a greater humanity

01:27:08.360 --> 01:27:11.490
than to feed of child at your breasts

01:27:11.490 --> 01:27:15.420
when your own child can be sold?

01:27:15.420 --> 01:27:18.010
Who else has a humanity to nurture

01:27:18.010 --> 01:27:21.280
and take care of the very
people who were killing them

01:27:21.280 --> 01:27:22.400
and were selling them off?

01:27:22.400 --> 01:27:25.100
There is something about
the humanity of black women

01:27:25.100 --> 01:27:26.990
that whether this country
acknowledges or not,

01:27:26.990 --> 01:27:28.090
I acknowledge it.

01:27:28.090 --> 01:27:30.130
I've seen it, we've got
the receipts to show that.

01:27:30.130 --> 01:27:32.720
We have been consistent in this country

01:27:32.720 --> 01:27:34.860
and so in that, that's why we do,

01:27:34.860 --> 01:27:37.773
one of our five principles
of Black Voters Matter

01:27:37.773 --> 01:27:41.500
is that we lift up the voices
and the work of black women.

